WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.980
IPC protocols need message framing.

2
00:00:03.080 --> 00:00:10.280
The receiver has to know where each message begins and ends before it can validate the content or dispatch an action.

3
00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:13.180
Some mechanisms preserve message boundaries.

4
00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:18.240
Others are byte streams and require the application to define a framing rule.

5
00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:20.660
The code should make that rule explicit.

6
00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:26.540
Partial reads, partial writes, and interrupted operations are normal conditions.

7
00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:28.800
A receiver might see only part of a header.

8
00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:32.260
A sender might write fewer bytes than requested.

9
00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:35.640
A peer might disconnect halfway through a message.

10
00:00:35.740 --> 00:00:41.260
Defensive code handles those states without treating incomplete input as a complete request.

11
00:00:41.540 --> 00:00:49.620
Length fields, delimiters, command identifiers, flags, versions, and payload formats all require validation before use.

12
00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:55.720
A declared length should be checked against maximum size, available bytes, and the schema for that message type.

13
00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:58.380
A command should be one the receiver supports.

14
00:00:58.480 --> 00:01:01.680
Flags should be accepted only when their meaning is defined.

15
00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:08.280
Validation should happen before allocation, copying, dispatch, or state-changing operations.

16
00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:19.100
If the receiver allocates based on an untrusted length, copies before checking the full range, or dispatches a command before validating the payload, the protocol boundary becomes fragile.

17
00:01:19.380 --> 00:01:21.460
Safe failure behavior matters.

18
00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:36.920
Malformed, unsupported, oversized, truncated, or out-of-sequence messages should produce predictable outcomes: reject the message, close the connection if appropriate, clean up partial state, and avoid changing trusted state.

19
00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:42.940
The parser should be boring in the best sense: explicit, bounded, and reviewable.
